---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import calendar from '../../assets/tetsu-calendar.png';
import progress from '../../assets/tetsu-progress.png';
import aiImport from '../../assets/tetsu-ai-import.png';
import timer from '../../assets/tetsu-workout-timer.png';
import restore from '../../assets/tetsu-backup-restore.png';
import logo from '../../assets/tetsu-logo.jpg';

const shot = { widths: [160, 320], sizes: '(min-width: 40rem) 8rem, 45vw' };
---

<Base
  title="Tetsu case study · David Salazar"
  description="How I framed, specified and shipped Tetsu, a workout planner and training log, with AI coding assistants under my direction."
  current="work"
>
  <div class="case">
    <header class="case-head">
<div class="col">
      <h1 data-reveal>Tetsu</h1>
      <p class="lede" data-reveal>
        A workout planner and training log. I owned the product, and AI coding assistants wrote the
        code under my direction.
      </p>
      <div class="brief" data-reveal>
        <p><strong>Problem</strong>People who lift keep routines in notes, and trackers make them retype everything.</p>
        <p><strong>What I did</strong>Defined the requirements, personas and acceptance criteria, and directed AI assistants to build it, backed by 75 tests, 61 browser cases and a 13-case validation matrix.</p>
        <p><strong>Result</strong>A live, installable app. Validation found three defects, and all three are fixed. Real usage numbers are not in yet.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="actions" data-reveal>
        <a class="btn" href="https://tetsugym.ai.studio" rel="noopener">Open the live app</a>
      </div>
      <Image class="logo" src={logo} alt="The Tetsu logo" widths={[400, 800]} sizes="24rem" data-reveal />
      <dl class="spec" data-reveal>
        <dt>What</dt>
        <dd>Workout planner and training log. Installable web app, English and Spanish.</dd>
        <dt>Role</dt>
        <dd>Solo. Product owner: problem, requirements, acceptance criteria, review and release decisions.</dd>
        <dt>Built with</dt>
        <dd>React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind, Express and the Gemini API on the server. Code written by Codex and Claude Code from my direction.</dd>
        <dt>Version</dt>
        <dd>1.5.0, as of October 14, 2026. Hosted on a free tier.</dd>
      </dl>
</div>
    </header>

    <section>
<div class="col">
      <h2 data-reveal>The problem</h2>
      <p data-reveal>
        People who lift seriously keep their routines in notes apps and spreadsheets. Commercial
        trackers ask them to re-enter everything through rigid templates, and they push one-rep-max
        numbers even for exercises like cable lateral raises, where a one-rep max means nothing.
        People either leave the tracker or stop logging.
      </p>
      <p data-reveal>
        Three working personas shaped what the app does. They were written for design decisions.
        They are not interview findings, and the project had no formal user research.
      </p>
      <div class="runin">
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>The Starter</strong> wants to train consistently but quits apps that open on a
          blank page. Starter templates, a month calendar and one-tap logging serve her.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>The Migrator</strong> has a year of routines in notes and fears losing his
          history. Paste-to-plan import with a review screen, exact units and decimals, and backup
          and restore serve him.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>The Progressor</strong> trains for 8 to 15 reps, often with no signal, and wants
          honest next-load advice. Planned-versus-actual logging, a conservative progression
          forecast and offline use serve her.
        </p>
      </div>
</div>
    </section>

    <section>
<div class="col">
      <h2 data-reveal>What I built</h2>
      <div class="stage" data-stage data-reveal>
        <ol class="stage-steps">
          <li data-screen="0">
            Paste-to-plan import with a review screen, so nothing replaces the current plan without
            the user seeing it first.
          </li>
          <li data-screen="1">A calendar with set-by-set logging and streaks.</li>
          <li data-screen="2">A rest timer that survives the phone sleeping.</li>
          <li data-screen="3">
            Progress forecasts using double progression: add reps until the top of the range, then
            add load, in real gym increments.
          </li>
          <li data-screen="4">
            Data safety and offline use: JSON backups, CSV exports, and an installable app that works
            without a signal.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <div class="stage-phone" aria-live="off">
          <div class="slides">
            <figure class="slide">
              <Image src={aiImport} alt="Tetsu’s import screen, where a pasted routine is reviewed before saving" widths={[320, 480, 640]} sizes="16rem" loading="lazy" />
              <figcaption>Import and review</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="slide">
              <Image src={calendar} alt="Tetsu’s month calendar on a phone" widths={[320, 480, 640]} sizes="16rem" loading="lazy" />
              <figcaption>Calendar</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="slide">
              <Image src={timer} alt="Tetsu’s workout screen with the rest timer" widths={[320, 480, 640]} sizes="16rem" loading="lazy" />
              <figcaption>Workout and rest timer</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="slide">
              <Image src={progress} alt="Tetsu’s progress tab, with history and advice for an exercise" widths={[320, 480, 640]} sizes="16rem" loading="lazy" />
              <figcaption>Progress and advice</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="slide">
              <Image src={restore} alt="Tetsu’s backup restore screen, showing a summary of the file and a choice between a safe merge and a full replace" widths={[320, 480, 640]} sizes="16rem" loading="lazy" />
              <figcaption>Backup and restore</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="shots five" data-reveal>
        <figure>
          <Image src={calendar} alt="Tetsu’s month calendar on a phone" {...shot} />
          <figcaption>Calendar</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <Image src={aiImport} alt="Tetsu’s import screen, where a pasted routine is reviewed before saving" {...shot} />
          <figcaption>Import and review</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <Image src={progress} alt="Tetsu’s progress tab, with history and advice for an exercise" {...shot} />
          <figcaption>Progress and advice</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <Image src={timer} alt="Tetsu’s workout screen with the rest timer" {...shot} />
          <figcaption>Workout and rest timer</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <Image src={restore} alt="Tetsu’s backup restore screen, showing a summary of the file and a choice between a safe merge and a full replace" {...shot} />
          <figcaption>Backup and restore</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="figure-note" data-reveal>Screens show made-up training data.</p>
</div>
    </section>

    <section>
<div class="col">
      <h2 data-reveal>Designing the AI import</h2>
      <p data-reveal>
        The import is where Tetsu uses AI in the product itself, so I specified it as a
        workflow with a person checking the result, not as a chatbot. This is the flow I defined.
      </p>
      <ol data-reveal>
        <li>The user pastes a routine, up to 12,000 characters.</li>
        <li>A local parser reads the common shapes first, so common formats don’t need the AI.</li>
        <li>
          Harder text goes to Gemini through a server I control. The key stays on the server, and
          the app tells the user their pasted text leaves the device.
        </li>
        <li>
          The result is checked on the server and again in the browser: at most 7 days and 30
          exercises per day, bounded sets and text, and any field I didn’t ask for is dropped.
        </li>
        <li>The user sees a review screen. Nothing replaces the current plan until they confirm.</li>
      </ol>
      <figure class="demo-gif" data-reveal>
        <button type="button" class="gif-toggle" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Import clip. Press to pause or play.">
          <img
            src="/tetsu-import.gif"
            width="320"
            height="696"
            alt="Tetsu’s import screen: a pasted routine is organized, then shown on a review screen with its weights intact, before anything is replaced"
            loading="lazy"
            data-poster="/tetsu-import-poster.png"
          />
        </button>
        <figcaption>A made-up routine, pasted, organized and reviewed. Waiting time is shortened. Tap the clip to pause.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="runin">
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Limits.</strong> 3 AI parses per minute and 15 per day per person, and a daily
          budget for the whole demo, because it runs on a free tier.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Failure is expected.</strong> If the AI is down or rate limited, manual entry,
          logging and timers keep working.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Measured.</strong> Every parse records success or failure, without the routine
          text, so the AI success rate can be tracked once there is real usage.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Tested without the AI.</strong> The tests for the import and the proxy’s security
          rules never call the real model, so they run the same every time.
        </p>
      </div>
</div>
    </section>

    <section>
<div class="col">
      <h2 data-reveal>My role</h2>
      <p data-reveal>
        I chose the problem, decided what to build and what to cut, wrote the acceptance criteria,
        reviewed every change, decided release timing, and defined what “done” means, including the
        validation and test evidence below. The code was written by AI coding assistants (Codex and
        Claude Code) working from my direction, in a pull-request workflow where each fix or
        feature had its own branch, tests and review. This is product ownership with AI-assisted
        implementation, not hand-written code.
      </p>
</div>
    </section>

    <section>
<div class="col">
      <h2 data-reveal>Decisions and trade-offs</h2>
      <div class="runin">
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Data stays in the browser, with no accounts.</strong> I considered a server
          database with sign-in. Keeping data local removes sign-up friction and the privacy
          burden, and the app works in a basement gym with no signal. The cost is no cross-device
          sync, so backups are a first-class feature.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Double progression instead of one-rep-max formulas.</strong> Those formulas come
          from low-rep barbell lifts. They mislead for 8 to 15 rep isolation work and ignore that
          dumbbells jump in fixed steps.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Forecasts are heuristics, and the app says so.</strong> Nothing here is validated
          against individual outcomes. Sparse, flat or declining histories produce “hold”, never
          automatic gains.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Review before replace on every import.</strong> AI output can be wrong. A
          reviewable preview keeps the user in control and keeps failures cheap.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Analytics are on by default, with a notice and a one-tap off.</strong> Strict
          opt-in would have produced almost no data. The trade-off is that default-on collection
          may not satisfy EU-style consent rules. I flagged that and documented it.
        </p>
      </div>
</div>
    </section>

    <section>
<div class="col">
      <details class="fold" data-reveal>
<summary><h2>Operations and security</h2></summary>
<div class="fold-body">
      <p data-reveal>
        The public demo is anonymous and uses a free-tier AI key, so protecting it was a real
        operations problem.
      </p>
      <ul data-reveal>
        <li>
          Usage guards: 3 AI parses per minute and 15 per day per client, 10 per minute and 100 per
          day overall, at most 2 concurrent jobs, and a daily model budget.
        </li>
        <li>
          Input and output bounds: 12,000 characters of routine text and 32&nbsp;KB request bodies. AI
          output is validated on the server and in the browser, and unknown fields are discarded.
        </li>
        <li>
          Trust boundaries: the server holds the key, requests can’t supply their own, origins are
          allowlisted exactly, and the production server serves only built assets.
        </li>
        <li>
          Graceful degradation: if the AI service fails or is rate limited, logging, timers and
          manual entry keep working.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p data-reveal>
        The limits are honest ones. In-memory limits reset on restart and apply per instance, and a
        determined attacker could still exhaust the free quota. The docs say what to add if that
        mattered.
      </p>
</div>
</details>
</div>
    </section>

    <section>
<div class="col">
      <details class="fold" data-reveal>
<summary><h2>Measurement</h2></summary>
<div class="fold-body">
      <p data-reveal>
        I instrumented the app to answer product questions, then built the dashboard myself in
        PostHog’s free tier. Events cover app opened, onboarding completed, workout completed,
        routine parsed (with AI success or failure), backup exported, app installed and progress
        viewed. The dashboard shows daily active users, an open to onboarded to first-workout
        funnel, retention, AI-parse success rate, workouts per week and feature usage.
      </p>
      <p data-reveal>
        Privacy was part of the design: a random per-browser ID, no routine content, weights, notes
        or names, a one-time notice, a Settings toggle that deletes the ID and any queued events,
        and a privacy page. Pasted routine text is the one thing that leaves the device, and only
        when the AI import is used. The interface says so.
      </p>
      <p data-reveal>
        Testing the instrumentation found a bug: a timing bug sent the same events dozens of
        times. I diagnosed it from the raw data, fixed it by serializing sends and giving each
        event a unique ID, and verified the fix against the live site with a scripted browser.
      </p>
      <div class="panel" data-reveal>
        <p>
          <strong>Usage results are not available yet.</strong> Development and test traffic was
          excluded on purpose, and the dashboard counts real usage from October 15, 2026. I’ll add
          figures here when there are real ones.
        </p>
      </div>
</div>
</details>
</div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
<div class="col">
      <h2 data-reveal>Validation</h2>
      <p data-reveal>
        I ran a 13-case matrix of synthetic training histories against the live app: full sessions,
        partial sessions, plateaus, declines, outliers, kg and lb conversion, edited prescriptions
        and bodyweight exercises. It found three real defects: a rounding error that disagreed with
        the advice text (1.25&nbsp;kg shown as 1.3), forecast points drawn for exercises where
        forecasting isn’t available, and advice that prescribed an average load nobody had
        actually lifted. All three are fixed and covered by tests.
      </p>
      <p data-reveal>
        The repository has 75 automated unit and API tests and 61 browser test cases. Every change
        shipped through its own branch and pull request, and audit passes produced fixes for
        data-loss risks before new features.
      </p>
      <p data-reveal>
        Not verified: behavior of iPhone locked-screen notifications, which depends on the
        platform.
      </p>
</div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
<div class="col">
      <h2 data-reveal>What I’d do next</h2>
      <p data-reveal>
        Shared rate limiting if usage grows beyond one instance, optional sign-in for cross-device
        sync, set-level analytics, and a decision on whether default-on analytics should become
        opt-in for EU visitors.
      </p>
</div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
<div class="col">
      <h2 data-reveal>What I learned</h2>
      <div class="runin">
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Measure early, then audit the measurement.</strong> The first dashboard data was
          wrong because of duplicate events. Looking at the raw data caught what the summary charts
          hid.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Write the limits down.</strong> Labeling heuristics, unverified platform behavior
          and the free-tier abuse ceiling made the product easier to trust and easier to defend in
          review.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Reversible beats tidy.</strong> Marking test data and keeping the old events cost
          nothing and avoided an irreversible mistake.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="actions" data-reveal>
        <a class="btn" href="https://tetsugym.ai.studio" rel="noopener">Open the live app</a>
        <a class="textlink" href="/#work">Back to work</a>
        <a class="textlink" href="/work/amazon/">Next: bottleneck analysis</a>
        <a class="textlink" href="/#contact">Get in touch</a>
      </div>
</div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script>
    // The screen beside the text follows the step in the middle of the viewport.
    const stage = document.querySelector('[data-stage]');
    if (stage && 'IntersectionObserver' in window) {
      const steps = [...stage.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.stage-steps li')];
      const slides = [...stage.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide')];
      const show = (i: number) => {
        steps.forEach((s, j) => s.classList.toggle('is-on', j === i));
        slides.forEach((s, j) => {
          s.classList.toggle('is-on', j === i);
          s.setAttribute('aria-hidden', String(j !== i));
        });
      };
      show(0);
      const band = new IntersectionObserver((es) => {
        es.forEach((e) => { if (e.isIntersecting) show(steps.indexOf(e.target as HTMLElement)); });
      }, { rootMargin: '-45% 0px -45% 0px' });
      steps.forEach((s) => band.observe(s));
    }
  </script>
  <script>
    // The clip plays on its own and a tap pauses it. People who ask for reduced motion start paused.
    const toggle = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('.gif-toggle');
    const img = toggle?.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>('img');
    if (toggle && img) {
      const gif = img.getAttribute('src') ?? '';
      const poster = img.dataset.poster ?? '';
      let playing = !matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
      const render = () => {
        img.src = playing ? gif + '?t=' + Date.now() : poster;
        toggle.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(!playing));
      };
      if (!playing) render();
      toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
        playing = !playing;
        render();
      });
    }
  </script>
</Base>
